WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.750
Many proteins are modified by the
addition of a phosphate chemical group.

2
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:05.540
This process is called phosphorylation.

3
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:07.470
Why does this occur?

4
00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:12.350
To function properly, cells must
regulate thousands of processes to

5
00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:16.680
occur at the right time and place. They
have this control primarily by

6
00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:21.410
regulating the activity of proteins.
One way proteins, like channels,

7
00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:25.050
enzymes and intercellular signalling
molecules, can be controlled is through

8
00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:25.460
phosphorylation.

9
00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:29.820
A phosphate group is added to the
protein by an enzyme called a kinase,

10
00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:33.520
which transfers a phosphate from ATP to
the target.

11
00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:38.810
This phosphate group can be removed by
a phosphatase enzyme, making

12
00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:43.090
phosphorylation, a process that can be
reversed. Whether or not a protein is

13
00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:47.140
phosphorylated depends on whether there
are more kinases or phosphatases near

14
00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:51.160
it. These levels can change within
seconds, which allows phosphoylation to

15
00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:55.140
rapidly change the target protein's
activity. But how does phosphorylation

16
00:00:55.730 --> 00:01:00.060
change the activity of a protein? First,
we need to understand a little about

17
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.930
what proteins are made of and how
they're structured. Proteins consist of

18
00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:07.890
chains of chemical subunits, called
amino acids, which fold up on each

19
00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:12.680
other to make a 3D structure. The amino
acids of a protein interact with one

20
00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:16.450
another to form a structure with a
specific shape, and it is that shape,

21
00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:20.980
which then determines the function of
the protein. Some of these amino acids

22
00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:24.870
can be modified by phosphorylation, and
the addition of a negatively charged

23
00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:28.870
phosphate group can affect how the
amino acids bind together. This can

24
00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:32.570
change the 3D structure of the protein,
and therefore, its function within a

25
00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:37.300
cell. All of these changes can modulate
cellular processes in many ways,

26
00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:41.500
sometimes speeding them up, or slowing
them down, or even stopping them

27
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:48.150
entirely. Cell motion, division,
metabolism, and even cell death are all

28
00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:49.230
regulated by phosphorylation.

